WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.
我是珍娜·费舍尔。

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.080
And I'm Angela Kinsey.
我是安吉拉·金赛。

3
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:07.360
We were on The Office together.
我们曾一起出演《办公室》。

4
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:08.620
And we're best friends.
我们也是最好的朋友。

5
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.
现在我们正为你们制作终极版《办公室》重温播客。

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.420
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.
每周，我们都会深度解析一集《办公室》，并分享只有身临其境的我们才能告诉你的独家幕后故事。

7
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office Ladies.
我们是“办公室女士”。

8
00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:25.950
On your mark, get set, go.
各就各位，预备，跑。

9
00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:28.290
Angela, it's a fun run.
安吉拉，这可是趣味长跑。

10
00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:32.610
Wait, I got a carb load, a bunch of fettuccine three seconds before I run.
等等，我得补充碳水，在起跑前三秒吃一大堆意大利宽面。

11
00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:34.470
Good idea.
好主意。

12
00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.550
Yeah, that's not going to turn on you.
是啊，那绝对不会让你出岔子的。

13
00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:43.330
Give me the creamy carbs seconds before I take off on a huge adventure.
在我开启一场大冒险前，给我来点奶油碳水吧。

14
00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:46.150
Guys, it is fun run part two.
伙计们，这是趣味长跑的第二部分。

15
00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:53.310
This is the very beginning of season four, written by Greg Daniels, directed by Greg Daniels.
这是第四季的开篇，由格雷格·丹尼尔斯编剧并执导。

16
00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:55.090
Let me hit you up with a summary.
让我来给你们做个总结。

17
00:00:55.550 --> 00:01:00.290
Last week, Michael hit Meredith with his car, breaking her pelvis.
上周，迈克尔开车撞伤了梅雷迪思，导致她骨盆骨折。

18
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:02.890
And we discovered, though,
但我们发现，

19
00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:11.990
that Meredith's trip to the hospital may have saved her life since it was revealed she had possibly been exposed to rabies and has now received a vaccine.
梅雷迪思的这次医院之行可能救了她一命，因为检查显示她可能接触过狂犬病毒，现在已经接种了疫苗。

20
00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:17.210
We also found out that sprinkles died on Dwight's watch.
我们还发现斯普林克尔斯是在德怀特的看管下死去的。

21
00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.430
On Dwight's watch?
在德怀特的看管下？

22
00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:29.530
Well, this week, this week, Michael organizes a fun run to raise money for rabies in honor of Meredith.
那么本周，迈克尔为了纪念梅雷迪思，组织了一场为狂犬病募捐的趣味长跑。

23
00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:33.110
And we find out what really happened to sprinkles.
我们也会揭晓斯普林克尔斯到底发生了什么。

24
00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:36.590
Do you see what I'm wearing in honor of this episode?
你看到我为了纪念这一集穿了什么吗？

25
00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:39.970
You're wearing your office lady's sprinkles sweatshirt.
你穿着你的“办公室女士”斯普林克尔斯卫衣。

26
00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:42.070
I am wearing my sprinkles sweatshirt.
没错，我穿着我的斯普林克尔斯卫衣。

27
00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:45.650
Well, lady, I did not wear anything in honor of sprinkles today.
好吧，女士，我今天没穿任何纪念斯普林克尔斯的衣服。

28
00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:46.130
Well, you know what?
好吧，你知道吗？

29
00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:48.670
That tracks for Pam in this episode.
这很符合帕姆在这一集里的表现。

30
00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:53.450
Because she helps, like, Angela, uh, uh, let's see, zero amount.
因为她对安吉拉的帮助，呃，怎么说呢，是零。

31
00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:54.330
Zero amount.
零帮助。

32
00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:55.610
Sorry.
抱歉。

33
00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:56.310
That's okay.
没关系。

34
00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:02.210
She's distracted because in this episode, it's also going to be revealed that Jim and Pam are dating.
她心不在焉，因为在这一集里，吉姆和帕姆正在约会的事实也将被揭露。

35
00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:10.410
Listen, one thing I thought of when I watched this episode was, wow, Happy Pam is a sassy Pam.
听着，我在看这一集时想到的第一件事就是，哇，快乐的帕姆真是个毒舌帕姆。

36
00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:14.030
She gives zero Fs about what's going on around her.
她对周围发生的事情完全不在乎。

37
00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:16.030
She's a totally different person.
她完全变了个人。

38
00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:17.290
Happy sass.
快乐的毒舌。

39
00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:18.470
Happy sass.
快乐的毒舌。

40
00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:20.090
Happy Pam sass.
快乐帕姆的毒舌。

41
00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:21.810
Happy Pam sass.
快乐帕姆的毒舌。

42
00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.430
Well, let's get into these fast facts.
好了，让我们进入快速事实环节。

43
00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:25.710
Fast fact number one.
快速事实第一条。

44
00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:31.570
You know, this episode is technically the second part of the episode that we broke down last week, right?
你知道，这一集严格来说是我们上周解析的那一集的下半部分，对吧？

45
00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:35.350
Because these episodes were an hour long.
因为这些剧集时长都是一小时。

46
00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:39.690
But if you notice, I feel like these episodes really stand on their own.
但如果你注意到了，我觉得这些剧集其实完全可以独立成篇。

47
00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:43.670
They kind of both had their own little plots, right?
它们各自都有自己的小情节，对吧？

48
00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:44.290
For sure.
确实如此。

49
00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:53.310
Well, I talked to Greg about that, and he told me that Kevin Riley requested four hour-long episodes to kick off season four.
我跟格雷格聊过这个，他告诉我凯文·赖利要求用四集一小时长的剧集来开启第四季。

50
00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:58.230
So the first four episodes of this season are all an hour long.
所以这一季的前四集时长都是一小时。

51
00:02:58.230 --> 00:02:59.630
Kevin loved our show.
凯文很喜欢我们的节目。

52
00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:03.390
But was there any other reason besides he was like, I want more office?
但除了他说“我想要更多《办公室》”之外，还有其他原因吗？

53
00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:13.790
I think it was just that would be a big announcement in the press, the return of the office, four hour-long episodes.
我觉得可能只是因为《办公室》的回归，加上四集一小时长的特别篇，这在媒体上会是个大新闻。

54
00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:18.610
Maybe also they didn't have something for the other time slot.
也许是因为他们当时没有其他节目来填补那个时段。

55
00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:21.150
So we were filling two time slots.
所以我们填补了两个时段。

56
00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:22.450
I'm speculating.
我是在瞎猜。

57
00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:22.970
I don't know.
我也不确定。

58
00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:26.410
I like your speculating face and tone.
我喜欢你瞎猜时的表情和语气。

59
00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:31.790
Because my husband does this sometimes when he wants to let the kids down easy.
因为我丈夫有时想委婉地拒绝孩子们时也会这样。

60
00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:37.510
Like, it's like, hey, guys, so about those pumpkins that we were going to get.
就像是，嘿，伙计们，关于我们要去买的那些南瓜。

61
00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.850
They exploded from the heat.
它们因为太热爆炸了。

62
00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:42.570
But, yeah.
不过，确实如此。

63
00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:46.690
Well, I was excited that we got these big, supersized episodes.
嗯，我很高兴我们能有这些超长版剧集。

64
00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:51.330
It meant that for the supporting cast, some of our storylines would definitely stay in.
这意味着对于配角来说，我们的一些故事情节肯定会被保留下来。

65
00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:52.330
And that was exciting.
这让人很兴奋。

66
00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:53.790
Yes.
没错。

67
00:03:54.350 --> 00:04:03.610
Well, Greg also told me that Kevin Riley told him they're only going to run in this hour-long length once at the beginning of the season.
格雷格还告诉我，凯文·赖利跟他说，这些剧集只会在季初以一小时的时长播出一次。

68
00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:06.710
Then we're going to have to rerun them later as half hours.
之后我们还得把它们剪成半小时的版本重播。

69
00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:09.530
And that's really why they stand alone.
这就是为什么它们能独立成篇的原因。

70
00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:20.890
Greg said it was a challenge because he had to wrap up the first episode in a way that would make it satisfying for a future viewer to watch it in a half-hour form.
格雷格说这很有挑战性，因为他必须以一种既能让未来观众在看半小时版本时感到满足的方式来收尾第一集。

71
00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:25.590
But he couldn't wrap it up too much for the people who were watching it as a one-hour episode.
但又不能收尾得太彻底，以免影响那些看一小时完整版剧集的观众。

72
00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:27.810
He said it was kind of a writing challenge.
他说这确实是个编剧难题。

73
00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:29.790
That sounds like a challenge.
听起来确实是个挑战。

74
00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:31.190
I think he did it, though.
但我认为他做到了。

75
00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:32.970
I think he crushed it.
我觉得他完成得非常出色。

76
00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:33.570
Greg Daniels.
格雷格·丹尼尔斯。

77
00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:34.580
He crushed it.
他完成得太棒了。

78
00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:35.730
You crushed it.
你完成得也很棒。

79
00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:42.130
Fast fact number two, lady, is a wardrobe breakdown with Carrie Bennett.
第二个速览事实，女士，是关于嘉莉·贝内特的服装解析。

80
00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:43.690
I love me some Carrie Bennett.
我超爱嘉莉·贝内特。

81
00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:44.490
Let's hear it.
让我们听听吧。

82
00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:54.310
We got so much mail about the wardrobe in this episode, which makes sense because we're seeing everybody out of their office attire.
我们收到了很多关于这一集服装的来信，这很合理，因为我们看到了大家脱下办公室装束的样子。

83
00:04:55.110 --> 00:05:00.950
Which Carrie said was always a challenge because she had created these office uniforms for people.
嘉莉说这总是一个挑战，因为她为每个人设计了这些办公室制服。

84
00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:07.790
So anytime we wore our, quote-unquote, street clothes, she was like, who are these people in their street clothes?
所以每当我们穿上所谓的“便装”时，她就会想，这些穿着便装的人是谁啊？

85
00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:10.070
Here's some of the mail we got.
以下是我们收到的一些来信。

86
00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:11.390
Alicia W.
艾丽西亚·W。

87
00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:16.550
and Amanda Mitchenfelder said, did you get to keep your shirts from Fun Run?
和阿曼达·米琴费尔德问，你们能留下《趣味跑》里的衬衫吗？

88
00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:21.710
And Emily Thomas wants to know, did you get to keep a Support the Rabid wristband?
还有艾米丽·托马斯想知道，你们能留下“支持狂犬病”的腕带吗？

89
00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:24.310
Yeah, I kept both.
是的，我两样都留下了。

90
00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:26.650
I kept my shirt and I kept my wristband.
我留下了我的衬衫，也留下了我的腕带。

91
00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:29.990
I had my wristband for a long time.
我的腕带留了很长一段时间。

92
00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:32.730
It's probably somewhere in my scrapbook bin.
它可能就在我的剪贴簿盒子里某处。

93
00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:35.470
My t-shirt, I did not keep.
我的T恤，我没留。

94
00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:45.230
Carrie kept it in case we ever needed Angela Martin to wear it again because we weren't sure how long this mourning process was going to go, you know?
嘉莉把它留下了，以防我们以后还需要安吉拉·马丁穿上它，因为我们不确定这个哀悼过程会持续多久，你懂的？

95
00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:52.070
Well, we should remind people, Angela, you did not wear one of the famous blue Fun Run t-shirts.
嗯，我们应该提醒大家，安吉拉，你当时并没有穿那件著名的蓝色《趣味跑》T恤。

96
00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:55.110
You wore a shirt with a picture of sprinkles on it.
你穿的是一件印有“洒糖”图案的衬衫。

97
00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:55.390
Yeah.
是的。

98
00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:58.070
Because people wrote in about this shirt too, Angela.
因为人们也写信问过这件衬衫的事，安吉拉。

99
00:05:58.330 --> 00:05:58.870
Molly C.
莫莉·C。

100
00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:06.850
and Isaiah Johnson asked, where did Angela's sprinkle shirt come from and did you get to keep it?
和以赛亚·约翰逊问，安吉拉的“洒糖”衬衫是从哪来的，你留下了吗？

101
00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:08.310
You said you did not keep it.
你说过你没留着它。

102
00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:09.650
I did not keep it.
我确实没留着。

103
00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:12.070
And Carrie made that shirt.
那件衬衫是嘉莉做的。

104
00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:13.310
She had that shirt made.
那是她让人做的。

105
00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:18.250
And also when I had my wardrobe fitting for this, Carrie and I talked and Angela would not be in shorts.
而且我试装的时候，嘉莉和我商量过，安吉拉不能穿短裤。

106
00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:20.230
She would not wear shorts.
她不会穿短裤的。

107
00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:22.650
So that's why I have sort of those little crop pants.
所以我才穿了那种七分裤。

108
00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:30.470
And then at first they had kids for me and I was like, I can't run in kids and I have to actually run.
起初他们给我准备了童装，我说我没法穿着童装跑步，而且我确实得跑。

109
00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:33.090
So I need an actual sneaker.
所以我需要一双真正的运动鞋。

110
00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:34.030
There you go.
这就对了。

111
00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:34.710
There you go.
没错。

112
00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:41.330
Well, Carrie told me about those blue shirts, the famous blue shirts with all the writing on them.
嘉莉跟我提过那些蓝色衬衫，就是上面写满字的那件著名的蓝色衬衫。

113
00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:44.770
So she designed those.
那是她设计的。

114
00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:45.970
We made them ourselves.
是我们自己做的。

115
00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:58.370
She said that she just tried to imagine Michael getting on the phone and ordering those t-shirts with the ridiculously long name and insisting that all the words be on the shirt.
她说她试着想象迈克尔打电话订购那些印着长得离谱的名字的T恤，还坚持要把所有字都印上去的样子。

116
00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:02.090
Which I think is a very funny way to approach that design.
我觉得这种设计思路非常有趣。

117
00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:04.350
I mean, I thought it was perfect.
我是说，我觉得这很完美。

118
00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:11.650
And honestly, while we're talking about the shirt, what the heck kind of budget did they have for this?
老实说，既然说到这件衬衫，他们拍这集到底有多少预算啊？

119
00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:16.790
Not only did they have bracelets and those t-shirts, they had flyers and banners.
他们不仅有手环和那些T恤，还有传单和横幅。

120
00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:21.710
They had a giant Meredith photo poster and a giant check.
他们还有一张巨大的梅雷迪思照片海报和一张巨额支票。

121
00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:23.630
What was their budget?
他们的预算到底是多少？

122
00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:31.570
I mean, we're going to see when we get to that scene in the conference room that we spend quite a bit of our donation money just throwing the fun run.
等我们看到会议室那场戏时就会发现，我们把相当一部分捐款都花在举办这场慈善跑上了。

123
00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:38.750
Carrie also told me that she chose the color blue for the shirts because everybody had to wear it and blue looks good on everyone.
嘉莉还告诉我，她选蓝色是因为每个人都得穿，而蓝色穿在谁身上都好看。

124
00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:43.010
She said she always applies the same logic for photo shoots and promo shoots.
她说她在拍摄照片和宣传片时总是遵循同样的逻辑。

125
00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:44.510
Blue just looks beSt.
蓝色就是最显眼。

126
00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:45.410
That's right.
没错。

127
00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:48.830
Well, let's talk about Dwight fan question from Ella Lewis.
好了，我们来聊聊艾拉·刘易斯提出的关于德怀特的粉丝问题。

128
00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:52.090
Why isn't Dwight wearing a fun run shirt?
为什么德怀特没穿慈善跑的衬衫？

129
00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:58.310
I feel like he would have been the most likely to support Michael's ideas, yet he's wearing a Shroot Farms shirt instead.
我觉得他应该是最支持迈克尔想法的人，但他却穿了一件施鲁特农场的衬衫。

130
00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:01.870
I think it makes perfect sense that he would self-advertise.
我觉得他给自己打广告完全说得通。

131
00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:03.130
I agree with Ella.
我同意艾拉的看法。

132
00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:07.950
I was surprised that Dwight would not just champion Michael the way he does.
我很惊讶德怀特竟然没有像往常那样支持迈克尔。

133
00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:10.670
Carrie told me that she made this shirt.
嘉莉告诉我，这件衬衫是她做的。

134
00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:18.950
She literally just used a standard stencil and she spray painted it in that parking lot right before we started shooting.
她真的只是用了一个标准模板，在开拍前就在停车场用喷漆喷出来的。

135
00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:21.390
But after the episode aired,
但在那集播出后，

136
00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:30.950
NBC marketing department got so many requests for that shirt that they called her and asked her for her art files so that they could mass produce it and sell it.
NBC市场部收到了大量关于那件衬衫的询问，他们打电话问她要设计文件，想批量生产并售卖。

137
00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:33.510
And Carrie was like, don't have an art file.
嘉莉说，我没有设计文件。

138
00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:37.890
Have a spray paint thing that I made in the parking lot.
我只有在停车场喷漆做出来的那个东西。

139
00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:41.330
If you want to come get my spray paint stencil.
如果你们想要，就来拿我的喷漆模板吧。

140
00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:42.550
Yeah.
是啊。

141
00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:44.550
She was like, okay.
她说，好吧。

142
00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:46.370
All right.
行吧。

143
00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:53.330
Well, our final question about the clothing comes from Ian MP and Marina Blank.
好了，关于服装的最后一个问题来自伊恩·MP和玛丽娜·布兰克。

144
00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:58.110
They're asking, is Michael wearing women's shorts for Fun Run?
他们问，迈克尔在慈善跑里穿的是女式短裤吗？

145
00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:00.130
Oh, man.
天哪。

146
00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:05.630
They're definitely some kind of like biker short with like the little windbreaker short over it.
那绝对是某种骑行短裤，外面套着那种防风短裤。

147
00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:06.630
Yeah.
没错。

148
00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:08.210
Carrie says no.
嘉莉说不是。

149
00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:10.430
They were not women's shorts.
那不是女式短裤。

150
00:09:10.810 --> 00:09:14.970
She said the thing with Steve was that he's actually really fit.
她说史蒂夫的情况是，他其实身材非常好。

151
00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:16.990
And Angela, you and I know this.
还有安吉拉，你我都心知肚明。

152
00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:20.510
He was really, really into fitness.
他真的非常非常热衷于健身。

153
00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:22.710
Well, he got into great shape.
嗯，他练就了非常棒的身材。

154
00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:25.550
We've talked about that for 40-year-old Virgin.
我们在聊《四十岁的老处男》时提到过这点。

155
00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:27.790
And then he has just made that part of his lifestyle.
后来他就把健身变成了自己生活方式的一部分。

156
00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:34.210
And I think in this episode, when he gets in his shorts and his t-shirt, you can see how fit he is.
我觉得在这一集里，当他穿上短裤和T恤时，你能看出他有多健壮。

157
00:09:34.330 --> 00:09:36.210
There's no hiding how fit he is.
他那健壮的身材根本藏不住。

158
00:09:36.370 --> 00:09:38.790
He has like 0% body fat.
他的体脂率几乎为零。

159
00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:39.790
And it's very clear.
这一点非常明显。

160
00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:46.930
Carrie said it was really a challenge to make him look like Michael anytime he was out of his sort of frumpy Michael suit.
凯莉说，只要他脱下那身松垮的迈克尔戏服，想让他看起来还像迈克尔简直是个巨大的挑战。

161
00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:50.330
Because he just was in such great shape.
因为他的身材实在太好了。

162
00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:52.930
She said it was just kind of difficult.
她说这真的挺难的。

163
00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:55.150
So there you go.
所以就是这样。

164
00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:56.470
Yeah.
没错。

165
00:09:57.570 --> 00:10:00.470
That is a costume breakdown.
这就是服装解析。

166
00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:01.710
I like it.
我喜欢。

167
00:10:01.870 --> 00:10:02.150
Thank you so much.
非常感谢。

168
00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:03.090
I like it.
我喜欢。

169
00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:03.710
All right.
好吧。

170
00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:05.230
Fast fact number three.
第三个冷知识。

171
00:10:06.790 --> 00:10:10.730
As you know, in this episode, Jim and Pam do not run.
如你所知，在这一集中，吉姆和帕姆没有跑步。

172
00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:12.130
A lot of people run.
很多人都在跑。

173
00:10:13.390 --> 00:10:14.790
But Jim and Pam do not.
但吉姆和帕姆没有。

174
00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:15.550
They walk.
他们是在走。

175
00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:16.970
They go to an estate sale.
他们去参加了一场遗产拍卖会。

176
00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:18.590
Jim and Pam don't run.
吉姆和帕姆没跑步。

177
00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:22.610
And Oscar and Stanley and Creed take a cab.
奥斯卡、斯坦利和克里德坐了出租车。

178
00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:24.230
But Jim and Pam do not run.
但吉姆和帕姆确实没跑。

179
00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:25.930
They walk in the back.
他们在后面走着。

180
00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:27.050
They hold hands.
他们牵着手。

181
00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:29.450
Well, there was a reason for this.
嗯，这背后是有原因的。

182
00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:30.150
I know.
我知道。

183
00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:36.930
The reason for this is because in May of 2007, we shot this in AuguSt.
原因在于，这一集是在2007年8月拍摄的，而我在5月受了伤。

184
00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:41.910
In May, I fell down a set of stairs in New York City.
5月份的时候，我在纽约市从楼梯上摔了下来。

185
00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:44.730
And I fractured four bones in my back.
我的背部骨折了四处。

186
00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:48.070
And I was not allowed to run.
医生不允许我跑步。

187
00:10:48.070 --> 00:10:56.650
And so the writers had to write a reason for me to only walk through this episode.
所以编剧们不得不写个理由，让我在这集里只能走路。

188
00:10:57.250 --> 00:10:58.570
That was such a scary night.
那晚真是太可怕了。

189
00:10:58.750 --> 00:10:59.370
I was with you.
当时我和你在一起。

190
00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:00.950
I know you were.
我知道你在。

191
00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:02.510
That was so scary.
那真的太吓人了。

192
00:11:02.930 --> 00:11:03.350
Yeah.
是啊。

193
00:11:03.550 --> 00:11:04.610
So here's what happened.
事情是这样的。

194
00:11:05.330 --> 00:11:06.570
It was May.
那是五月。

195
00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:07.850
And we were in New York.
当时我们在纽约。

196
00:11:07.850 --> 00:11:09.510
And we were promoting the office.
我们在为《办公室》做宣传。

197
00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:11.570
We were promoting the season finale.
我们在宣传季终集。

198
00:11:11.950 --> 00:11:15.790
And they threw this big party, in quotes, party.
他们举办了一个盛大的派对，所谓的“派对”。

199
00:11:15.790 --> 00:11:18.830
It was a work event at this club called Budokan.
那是在一家叫Budokan的夜店举办的工作活动。

200
00:11:19.010 --> 00:11:19.130
Yeah.
没错。

201
00:11:19.210 --> 00:11:22.490
It was our job to kind of hobnob with advertisers and stuff.
我们的工作就是去和广告商之类的应酬。

202
00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:23.710
We were at work.
我们在工作。

203
00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:25.510
But it was a fancy work.
但那是一份体面的工作。

204
00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:26.550
Yes.
是的。

205
00:11:26.810 --> 00:11:28.450
So we're all dressed up.
所以我们都盛装打扮。

206
00:11:28.610 --> 00:11:31.590
And this club is two floors.
那个俱乐部有两层楼。

207
00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:33.490
So you enter on the upper floor.
你是从楼上进去的。

208
00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:35.630
They had this little bar with drinks.
那里有个小吧台提供饮料。

209
00:11:35.850 --> 00:11:40.510
And they were serving these, like, pineapple-y, daiquiri things.
他们供应那种类似菠萝味的代基里酒。

210
00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:42.390
And it was sweet.
味道很甜。

211
00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:43.990
And I was like, this seems great.
我当时心想，这看起来不错。

212
00:11:43.990 --> 00:11:45.150
So I grabbed one.
于是我拿了一杯。

213
00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:50.190
But then to get to kind of the main part of the event, you had to go down this long set
但要到达活动的主会场，你得走下一长串

214
00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:52.690
of stairs that were marble.
大理石楼梯。

215
00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:54.350
And they were steep, you guys.
伙计们，那楼梯真的很陡。

216
00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:57.130
They had a really deep pitch to them.
坡度非常大。

217
00:11:57.350 --> 00:11:57.630
Yeah.
是啊。

218
00:11:57.710 --> 00:11:58.410
They're still there.
它们还在那里。

219
00:11:58.550 --> 00:11:59.570
This club is still there.
那个俱乐部还在那里。

220
00:11:59.950 --> 00:12:01.610
So I'm walking down these stairs.
我走下那些楼梯时。

221
00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:03.310
I've basically just arrived.
我基本上才刚到。

222
00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:06.090
I've taken one sip of my little daiquiri.
我才抿了一口我的小杯代基里酒。

223
00:12:06.710 --> 00:12:08.010
And something happens.
然后意外就发生了。

224
00:12:08.170 --> 00:12:10.250
My foot slips out from underneath me.
我的脚下一滑。

225
00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:14.030
And I go crashing down on the edge of these stairs hard.
我重重地摔在楼梯边缘上。

226
00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:17.730
To add insult to injury, I also threw my drink in my own face.
更糟糕的是，我还把酒泼了自己一脸。

227
00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:19.490
So that was fun.
那可真是太“有趣”了。

228
00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:21.510
So I'm sitting on the ground.
我坐在地上。

229
00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:24.050
I'm half covered in daiquiri.
浑身沾满了代基里酒。

230
00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:30.210
And I immediately start to feel like I'm going to vomit or everything gets dizzy.
我立刻感到一阵恶心，或者说天旋地转。

231
00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:36.410
And I remember this bouncer comes over and picked me up.
我记得有个保安走过来把我扶了起来。

232
00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:43.350
And he was treating me like I was like a boozy, drunken Hollywood starlet.
他对待我的样子，就像我是个喝得烂醉的好莱坞小明星。

233
00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:47.230
Like he was in no way regarding me with much care.
他完全没打算对我温柔一点。

234
00:12:47.410 --> 00:12:48.510
It was like, get her out of there.
就像是说，把她弄出去。

235
00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:49.650
Get the boozer out.
把那个酒鬼弄走。

236
00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:55.050
And I was like, I'm not, I don't, but I was all disoriented, you know?
我当时想说，我不是，我没有，但我整个人都晕头转向的，你懂吗？

237
00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:59.190
So I remember they took me back to the storeroom, Angela, and you were there.
我记得他们把我带到了储藏室，安吉拉，你当时也在那儿。

238
00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:02.070
And I told you I didn't feel good.
我告诉你我感觉很不舒服。

239
00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:05.850
And I remember you lifted up the back of my shirt.
我记得你掀起了我的衬衫后背。

240
00:13:06.950 --> 00:13:09.590
And you said, call an ambulance.
然后你说，快叫救护车。

241
00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:14.330
Well, I was behind you on that staircase and I saw you go down.
嗯，我当时就在你身后的楼梯上，我看着你摔了下去。

242
00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:15.790
It happened in slow motion.
那过程就像慢动作一样。

243
00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:21.070
And someone had spilt their drink or something on those stairs and they were marble and they
有人把饮料洒在了楼梯上，那是大理石材质的，所以

244
00:13:21.070 --> 00:13:21.830
were slick.
非常滑。

245
00:13:22.090 --> 00:13:27.190
And you, you had these wedge shoes on, you turned your ankle and you went down so hard.
你当时穿着坡跟鞋，脚一崴，摔得特别重。

246
00:13:27.310 --> 00:13:32.030
And you know, when you're the person watching something happen, how you're like, nah, like
你知道的，当你作为旁观者目睹这一切时，你会觉得，不，就像

247
00:13:32.030 --> 00:13:33.390
I didn't know how to stop it.
我不知道该怎么阻止它。

248
00:13:33.450 --> 00:13:34.730
It was all in motion.
一切都在发生中。

249
00:13:36.190 --> 00:13:41.410
And yeah, I'll never forget that night because your back was, it looked bad.
是啊，我永远不会忘记那个晚上，因为你的背部，看起来伤得很重。

250
00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:43.650
And yeah, I was, I was scared.
而且，我当时真的被吓坏了。

251
00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:45.910
Well, I don't remember much else after that.
嗯，在那之后的事我就记不太清了。

252
00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:49.610
I don't remember the ambulance ride, but I know you were with me, Ange.
我不记得坐救护车的过程，但我知道你一直陪着我，安吉。

253
00:13:49.710 --> 00:13:54.610
And I remember going in and out at the hospital and you were by my side the whole time, lady.
我记得在医院里意识时有时无，但你一直守在我身边，亲爱的。

254
00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:55.250
I know.
我知道。

255
00:13:55.250 --> 00:13:55.830
You were there.
你当时就在那儿。

256
00:13:56.630 --> 00:13:59.010
And I remember they x-rayed me.
我还记得他们给我拍了X光。

257
00:14:00.210 --> 00:14:06.030
I remember you told me the guy in the bed next to me was handcuffed to his bed.
我记得你告诉我，我隔壁床的那个人被铐在床上。

258
00:14:06.030 --> 00:14:10.430
There was a guy in a chair next to your bed and they brought him in.
当时你床边有把椅子，他们带了个人进来。

259
00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:14.550
He was real surly and they handcuffed him to the chair.
那人脾气很暴躁，他们就把他铐在椅子上了。

260
00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:17.870
And I shut the little curtain around your bed.
我就把你床边的小帘子拉上了。

261
00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:18.690
Oh dear.
天哪。

262
00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:22.250
And also I was staying with you and I was really adamant about it.
而且我当时一直陪着你，态度非常坚决。

263
00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:25.130
You know, I was like, she has no family here.
你知道，我就想，她在这儿没亲人。

264
00:14:25.450 --> 00:14:28.130
You, I am not like going anywhere.
你，我是哪儿也不会去的。

265
00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:31.590
She's in an emergency room in a city that's not her own.
她身处一个陌生城市的急诊室里。

266
00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:33.010
She has no family.
她没有家人。

267
00:14:33.010 --> 00:14:35.090
And I, you are stuck with me, buddy.
所以，你只能忍受我了，伙计。

268
00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:38.170
So they, um, they didn't mess with me.
所以他们，嗯，他们也没敢赶我走。

269
00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:41.070
And I stayed with you till four in the morning.
我一直陪你到凌晨四点。

270
00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:46.550
We got you a hospital room and, um, we knew more details then.
我们给你办了住院，嗯，那时候我们也了解了更多细节。

271
00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:51.250
And once we got you set up in your room, I went, I was supposed to be on the Today Show
等你住进病房后，我本该去参加《今日秀》节目，

272
00:14:51.250 --> 00:14:53.690
that morning, um, with Rainn Wilson.
就在那天早上，嗯，和雷恩·威尔森一起。

273
00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:58.090
We were supposed to be interviewed for the office and they had a whole piece about Dwight
我们本该接受采访谈谈《办公室》，他们还准备了一段关于德怀特

274
00:14:58.090 --> 00:14:58.530
and Angela.
和安吉拉的专题。

275
00:14:59.150 --> 00:15:02.990
And I asked NBC, you know, I was like, I'm supposed to be on the air.
我跟NBC说，你知道，我说，我本该上节目的。

276
00:15:02.990 --> 00:15:05.770
In an hour, but I'm in a hospital room with my friend.
再过一小时就要录了，但我现在在医院陪我的朋友。

277
00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:07.650
I can't, I can't be there.
我没法，我没法过去。

278
00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:09.570
And they said, don't worry.
他们说，别担心。

279
00:15:09.730 --> 00:15:10.930
We'll let the Today Show know.
我们会通知《今日秀》的。

280
00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:15.150
So years later, I'm at the White House Correspondents Dinner.
多年后，我在白宫记者协会晚宴上。

281
00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:18.550
I'm sitting at the NBC Today Show table.
我坐在NBC《今日秀》的桌旁。

282
00:15:19.210 --> 00:15:22.930
And we talk about, we're just talking about, oh, when was the last time we saw you?
我们聊起，就在聊，哦，上次见你是什么时候？

283
00:15:23.350 --> 00:15:26.970
And they were like, you were supposed to do the Today Show with Rainn Wilson.
他们说，你本该和雷恩·威尔森一起上《今日秀》的。

284
00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:28.230
You were a no-show.
你当时没来。

285
00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:29.950
And I was like, what?
我说，什么？

286
00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:31.650
I wasn't a no-show.
我不是没去。

287
00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:34.750
I was with Jenna in a hospital.
我当时在医院陪珍娜。

288
00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:36.550
Didn't they tell you that?
他们没告诉你吗？

289
00:15:36.710 --> 00:15:37.790
They never told them.
他们从来没告诉过他们。

290
00:15:38.210 --> 00:15:39.050
I just didn't.
我只是没去成。

291
00:15:39.150 --> 00:15:40.910
They never told them why you didn't show up.
他们从来没告诉过他们你为什么没出现。

292
00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:42.830
They just thought you were like a flaky actress.
他们只觉得你是个不靠谱的女演员。

293
00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:49.270
This night, branded me in this bouncer's eyes as some boozed-up actress.
那一晚，在那个保镖眼里，我被贴上了酗酒女演员的标签。

294
00:15:49.570 --> 00:15:52.710
And then the Today Show, the next day, thought you were a flake.
然后《今日秀》第二天也觉得你是个不靠谱的人。

295
00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:54.630
And we were like, no, tragedy.
我们当时就想，不，是悲剧。

296
00:15:55.270 --> 00:15:56.230
Tragedy happened.
发生了悲剧。

297
00:15:56.530 --> 00:15:56.810
Yeah.
是啊。

298
00:15:56.950 --> 00:15:58.650
And I've done the Today Show now since.
后来我也上过《今日秀》了。

299
00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:02.150
But I was like, how many years did they just think I didn't show up?
但我当时就在想，他们到底觉得我无故缺席了多少年？

300
00:16:03.350 --> 00:16:04.410
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

301
00:16:12.830 --> 00:16:13.770
You're going to walk again.
你还能重新走路。

302
00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:14.790
Oh, my God.
天哪。

303
00:16:14.970 --> 00:16:16.190
And I was like, that's the good.
当时我就想，这可是好事。

304
00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:17.610
I mean, that is good news.
我是说，这确实是好消息。

305
00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:21.650
But that sounds like there's a lot of bad news coming.
但听起来后面还有很多坏消息。

306
00:16:22.630 --> 00:16:27.570
So as it turns out, I had fractured four of the transverse vertebrae in my back.
结果发现，我背部的四个横突椎骨骨折了。

307
00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:33.170
If you're going to have a back injury, I guess this is the best one because they heal on their own,
如果你非要受背伤，我想这算是最好的情况了，因为它们能自愈，

308
00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:35.010
similar to a broken rib.
就像断了根肋骨一样。

309
00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:37.770
And motion is the best thing for you.
而且多活动对你最有好处。

310
00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:42.690
So even though walking and moving was really difficult for me, I was encouraged to do it.
所以尽管当时走路和活动对我来说非常困难，但我还是被鼓励去尝试。

311
00:16:43.690 --> 00:16:46.010
Here's a crazy twist to the story.
故事还有个疯狂的转折。

312
00:16:46.410 --> 00:16:54.910
You know, NBC had put us up at the Ritz-Carlton for these two days that we were going to do press.
你知道，NBC安排我们住在丽思卡尔顿酒店，为期两天，我们要在那儿做宣传。

313
00:16:55.090 --> 00:16:59.210
They had a deal with the hotel, and the entire cast was put up at the Ritz-Carlton.
他们和酒店有协议，整个剧组都住在丽思卡尔顿。

314
00:16:59.350 --> 00:17:01.830
And remember, lady, we asked for adjoining rooMs.
还记得吗，女士，我们当时要求住连通房。

315
00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:03.670
And we were so excited.
我们当时兴奋极了。

316
00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:06.030
Talk about country mouse come to town.
简直就像乡下老鼠进城一样。

317
00:17:06.030 --> 00:17:09.830
We're at the Ritz-facing Central Park.
我们住在丽思卡尔顿，正对着中央公园。

318
00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:11.410
Two whole nights.
整整两个晚上。

319
00:17:11.590 --> 00:17:12.910
We were so excited.
我们当时太激动了。

320
00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:15.390
Well, I couldn't go home.
好吧，我没法回家。

321
00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:16.410
I couldn't leave.
我没法离开。

322
00:17:16.610 --> 00:17:23.370
So NBC had to pay for me to live at the Ritz-Carlton for two weeks.
所以NBC不得不出钱让我住在丽思卡尔顿两周。

323
00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:25.690
And guess what?
你猜怎么着？

324
00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:26.690
What?
怎么了？

325
00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:29.570
For the first week, I was with you the whole time.
第一周，我一直和你在一起。

326
00:17:29.910 --> 00:17:30.550
I know.
我知道。

327
00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:31.630
You didn't leave my side.
你一步都没离开过我。

328
00:17:31.790 --> 00:17:35.170
I didn't leave your side because you had family on the way.
我没离开你是因为你的家人正在赶来的路上。

329
00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:38.690
But, you know, they needed a few days to get there.
但你知道，他们需要几天时间才能到。

330
00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:40.010
And so I was there.
所以我就在那儿陪着你。

331
00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:42.770
And I just remember that time.
我至今还记得那段时光。

332
00:17:43.030 --> 00:17:49.130
And, you know, I think every friendship has these milestone moments that you're forever bonded by.
你知道，我觉得每段友谊都有一些里程碑式的时刻，让你们从此紧密相连。

333
00:17:49.270 --> 00:17:50.630
And we've certainly had a few.
我们当然也有过几次这样的时刻。

334
00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:54.230
And best friends listening to this out there, you know those moments.
正在听节目的好朋友们，你们一定懂那种时刻。

335
00:17:54.450 --> 00:17:58.790
This was one of those moments when you're helping your friend get on the toilet.
这就是那种当你帮朋友上厕所时的时刻。

336
00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:02.730
And Jenna, you did this for me after my C-section.
珍娜，我剖腹产后你就是这么照顾我的。

337
00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:03.650
You helped me.
你帮了我。

338
00:18:03.850 --> 00:18:05.330
Like, I remember this.
我记得很清楚。

339
00:18:05.450 --> 00:18:08.110
These were, this was one of those BFF moments.
这些就是那种闺蜜时刻。

340
00:18:08.910 --> 00:18:15.250
We have both been with the other while one has had a catheter.
我们俩都曾陪着对方插过导尿管。

341
00:18:15.530 --> 00:18:15.970
Yeah.
没错。

342
00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:19.010
Do you need your friend to step out while I give you this catheter?
“你需要朋友先出去，好让我给你插导尿管吗？”

343
00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:19.550
Nah.
“不用。”

344
00:18:20.030 --> 00:18:23.030
No, I'd actually prefer to hold her hand, please.
“不，我其实更希望她能握着我的手，拜托了。”

345
00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:23.370
Yeah.
没错。

346
00:18:25.150 --> 00:18:26.910
I'd like someone here with me.
“我希望有人陪着我。”

347
00:18:27.050 --> 00:18:27.190
Yeah.
没错。

348
00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:33.610
So anybody out there, if you're wondering why Angela and I are best friends and will be best friends forever,
所以各位，如果你们好奇为什么安吉拉和我能成为好朋友，并且会永远是好朋友，

349
00:18:35.130 --> 00:18:38.050
friends who catheter together stay together, all right?
一起插过导尿管的闺蜜才能长久，懂了吗？

350
00:18:38.230 --> 00:18:38.870
Oh, man.
天哪。

351
00:18:38.870 --> 00:18:47.190
Oh, my final piece of this story is simply that I couldn't shower for several days and my hair was so smelly.
哦，关于这个故事，最后一点就是我好几天没法洗澡，头发臭得要命。

352
00:18:47.310 --> 00:18:49.170
I'm sorry you had to live with that, Ange.
真抱歉你得忍受那种日子，安吉。

353
00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:53.010
That pineapple, it really turned real gross.
那个菠萝，真的变得特别恶心。

354
00:18:53.890 --> 00:18:59.690
And I did wet, I did wet a wash rag and try to dampen your hair with the wash rag.
而且我确实弄湿了，我弄湿了一条毛巾，试着帮你擦擦头发。

355
00:19:00.070 --> 00:19:00.650
You did.
你确实这么做了。

356
00:19:00.830 --> 00:19:01.310
You did.
你确实做了。

357
00:19:01.470 --> 00:19:05.730
But anyway, guys, that's why Pam doesn't run in this episode.
但总之，各位，这就是为什么帕姆在这一集里没有跑步的原因。

358
00:19:05.730 --> 00:19:12.210
I was healed, but I don't think at this point I was driving myself yet and I wasn't allowed to run.
我当时已经痊愈了，但我觉得那时候我还不能开车，也不被允许跑步。

359
00:19:12.370 --> 00:19:19.510
Your doctor had you on a very specific sort of recovery plan because you do not mess with the back.
你的医生为你制定了非常具体的康复计划，因为背部问题可不能马虎。

360
00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:21.370
You don't mess around.
绝对不能马虎。

361
00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:23.310
But I'm fully healed now.
不过我现在已经完全康复了。

362
00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:24.070
No probleMs.
没问题。

363
00:19:24.310 --> 00:19:25.550
No residual probleMs.
没有任何后遗症。

364
00:19:26.590 --> 00:19:29.390
Well, I loved the storyline for Jim and Pam.
嗯，我很喜欢吉姆和帕姆的那条故事线。

365
00:19:29.590 --> 00:19:31.150
I thought it made it just really sweet.
我觉得它让剧情变得非常温馨。

366
00:19:31.310 --> 00:19:33.270
Like they were just going to hang out.
就像他们只是打算去逛逛。

367
00:19:33.450 --> 00:19:34.510
They weren't going to participate.
他们没打算参加比赛。

368
00:19:34.510 --> 00:19:41.150
And then they had this sort of lovely walk and they got to hold hands and I thought it worked out great.
然后他们就这样散了散步，还牵了手，我觉得效果很棒。

369
00:19:41.450 --> 00:19:42.370
It worked.
效果很好。

370
00:19:42.570 --> 00:19:43.310
It worked.
效果很好。

371
00:19:44.150 --> 00:19:48.970
Well, guys, thanks for listening to us go down that memory lane.
好了，各位，感谢收听我们回顾往事。

372
00:19:49.230 --> 00:19:53.370
We'll take a break and we'll be back to really dive into this episode.
我们要休息一下，回来后将深入探讨这一集的内容。

373
00:19:53.710 --> 00:20:04.220
Okay, we are back.
好的，我们回来了。

374
00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:07.460
This episode opens with Pam answering the phone.
这一集开头是帕姆在接电话。

375
00:20:07.460 --> 00:20:14.880
Michael Scott's Dunder Mifflin Scranton Meredith Palmer Memorial Celebrity Rabies Awareness Fun Run Race for the Cure.
迈克尔·斯科特的邓德·米福林斯克兰顿分公司梅雷迪思·帕尔默纪念名人狂犬病意识趣味长跑慈善赛。

376
00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:18.340
But it's, you know, it's the Pro-Am Race for the Cure.
但其实，你知道，那是业余职业混合慈善赛。

377
00:20:18.520 --> 00:20:21.940
My favorite thing about that whole moment was how you delivered.
关于整个片段，我最喜欢的就是你的演绎方式。

378
00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:23.200
They hung up.
他们挂断了。

379
00:20:26.480 --> 00:20:27.100
Good.
很好。

380
00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:28.280
It's so good.
太棒了。

381
00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:37.180
Well, we got some fan questions about this from Gabriel B, Frank F, Kalos D, Alberto R, and Camille Y.
嗯，我们收到了一些粉丝关于此事的提问，来自加布里埃尔·B、弗兰克·F、卡洛斯·D、阿尔贝托·R和卡米尔·Y。

382
00:20:37.540 --> 00:20:42.180
Jenna, did you have to memorize that long title for the Fun Run Race for the Cure?
珍娜，你必须背下那个超长的“趣味长跑慈善赛”名称吗？

383
00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:44.860
Or did you look at a piece of paper every time you said it?
还是说你每次说的时候都要看一眼纸条？

384
00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:47.700
No, you guys, I had to memorize that.
不，伙计们，我必须把它背下来。

385
00:20:47.800 --> 00:20:50.960
But I did have it sitting on a piece of paper in front of me.
但我确实在面前放了一张纸条。

386
00:20:50.960 --> 00:20:55.300
Like, I would look at it intensely because that is a mouthful.
我会盯着它看，因为那名字实在太拗口了。

387
00:20:55.480 --> 00:20:56.880
That is a lot to have to say.
要说出那一长串话真的很不容易。

388
00:20:57.040 --> 00:20:57.200
Yeah.
是的。

389
00:20:57.300 --> 00:20:59.480
But I did memorize it for that scene.
但我确实为那一幕把它背下来了。

390
00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:01.840
And it's a mouthful that doesn't make sense.
而且那串话听起来根本没逻辑。

391
00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:04.820
Like, for me, I can memorize something if it makes sense.
对我来说，如果内容有逻辑，我就能记住。

392
00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:06.800
My brain will piece it together for me.
我的大脑会自动帮我把它们串联起来。

393
00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:09.940
But if it's just like a list, you have to work at it.
但如果只是一堆名词列表，你就得下苦功了。

394
00:21:10.420 --> 00:21:10.680
Yeah.
没错。

395
00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:13.440
That is more difficult for sure.
那确实更难。

396
00:21:13.740 --> 00:21:18.080
Like, there's no way I could have ever been one of those doctors on ER or whatever,
就像我绝对不可能去演《急诊室的故事》之类的剧里的医生，

397
00:21:18.080 --> 00:21:21.900
like on Grey's Anatomy, where they're running down the hallway and they got someone on the
或者像《实习医生格蕾》那样，他们一边在走廊里跑，一边推着担架，

398
00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:26.640
stretcher and they're like, we need three BPUs and get the suture.
嘴里还喊着：我们需要三个BPU，快拿缝合线来。

399
00:21:26.960 --> 00:21:35.580
All the time when I watch those shows.
我每次看那些剧时都会这么想。

400
00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:36.720
All the time.
每次都是。

401
00:21:36.720 --> 00:21:39.200
I think like they're just yelling out jargon.
我觉得他们只是在乱喊专业术语。

402
00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:41.240
They have no idea what they're saying.
他们根本不知道自己在说什么。

403
00:21:41.600 --> 00:21:42.000
Yes.
没错。

404
00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:42.460
Yeah.
是啊。

405
00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:47.900
Well, then Michael has an amazing talking head that I really love, where he lets us know
好吧，迈克尔有个我很喜欢的采访镜头，他在里面告诉我们

406
00:21:47.900 --> 00:21:51.740
that a woman should not have to be hit by a car to learn that she has rapies.
女人不该非得被车撞了才知道自己得了狂犬病。

407
00:21:51.900 --> 00:21:52.920
But here we are.
但事实就是这样。

408
00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:53.940
Well, you know what?
好吧，你知道吗？

409
00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:56.140
Michael is there fighting for women once again.
迈克尔又一次站出来为女性抗争了。

410
00:21:56.900 --> 00:21:58.160
Yes, exactly.
没错，正是如此。

411
00:21:59.680 --> 00:22:01.760
Well, then we go out into the bullpen.
接着我们来到办公区。

412
00:22:01.900 --> 00:22:02.780
We're panning around.
镜头扫过全场。

413
00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:09.200
People are, I guess, on the phones, kind of phone banking, trying to get donations for
我想大家都在打电话，算是电话募捐吧，试图为

414
00:22:09.200 --> 00:22:10.040
this cause.
这个事业筹集捐款。

415
00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:13.120
He has the whole office doing this.
他让整个办公室的人都参与进来。

416
00:22:13.280 --> 00:22:17.080
It's unbelievable that any of us had a job after this day.
真不敢相信那天之后我们居然还没被开除。

417
00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:21.260
And I love that he walked up and said, hi, Stankly.
我特别喜欢他走过去说，嘿，斯坦利。

418
00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:25.640
Like, when is Michael going to learn you can't do bits with Stanley?
迈克尔到底什么时候才能学会，你不能跟斯坦利开这种玩笑？

419
00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:27.320
Stanley doesn't want your bits.
斯坦利才不吃你这套。

420
00:22:27.580 --> 00:22:28.580
He doesn't.
他确实不吃。

421
00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:31.120
He is, Stanley is a bit free person.
他，斯坦利是个对这种玩笑免疫的人。

422
00:22:31.420 --> 00:22:32.820
Don't bring your bits to Stanley.
别拿你的那些把戏去烦斯坦利。

423
00:22:33.300 --> 00:22:35.880
Well, Phyllis is hard at work on the rabies quilt.
菲利斯正在努力赶制狂犬病拼布被。

424
00:22:36.180 --> 00:22:37.000
She holds it up.
她把它举了起来。

425
00:22:37.160 --> 00:22:39.480
How many quilts does this company make?
这家公司到底要做多少床拼布被？

426
00:22:39.640 --> 00:22:44.160
I mean, I felt like this was a callback to the depression quilt from safety training.
我是说，我觉得这呼应了“安全培训”那一集里的抑郁症拼布被。

427
00:22:44.640 --> 00:22:46.980
Any wonder Dwight was like, should we do a depression quilt?
难怪德怀特会问，我们要不要做个抑郁症拼布被？

428
00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:49.060
And Michael's like, no, no time to sew the quilt.
迈克尔却说，不，没时间缝被子了。

429
00:22:49.060 --> 00:22:50.840
Because clearly it's a thing.
因为显然这已经成了一个梗。

430
00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:52.280
It's a thing.
确实是个梗。

431
00:22:52.400 --> 00:22:53.300
Put Phyllis on it.
交给菲利斯去做就行。

432
00:22:54.060 --> 00:22:56.540
Well, we had a fan question from Mary Halsey.
我们收到一个来自玛丽·哈尔西的粉丝提问。

433
00:22:56.720 --> 00:22:58.280
Who made the rabies quilt?
狂犬病拼布被是谁做的？

434
00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:00.940
Well, guys, it was Phil Shea.
伙计们，是菲尔·谢做的。

435
00:23:01.140 --> 00:23:05.180
I mean, he didn't personally make it, but he had to get someone to make those squares.
我是说，不是他亲手缝的，但他得找人把那些方块拼起来。

436
00:23:05.440 --> 00:23:11.260
And if you want to buy it, there is a full-size version of the quilt online.
如果你想买的话，网上有全尺寸版本的拼布被。

437
00:23:11.440 --> 00:23:12.220
Just Google it.
直接谷歌搜一下就行。

438
00:23:12.540 --> 00:23:12.900
Get out.
别逗了。

439
00:23:12.920 --> 00:23:14.220
Rabies quilt the office.
搜“办公室 狂犬病拼布被”。

440
00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:15.580
People are making them.
真的有人在做这个。

441
00:23:15.700 --> 00:23:16.520
You can buy them.
